WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:11.880
Microservices architecture is a pattern for organizing computer systems into services that can scale with demand

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:20.180
Back in the 1990's an internet company would run a big monolithic program on a server that the company maintained

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:21.820
on-premise

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:27.629
To serve an increase in traffic a popular company would simply add more instances of the monolith

00:00:28.599 --> 00:00:31.829
Monolithic architectures do have some positive features

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:36.119
A monolith centralizes the codebase so it is in one place

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:40.590
Engineers can step through any part of the code when they are debugging

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:48.780
Also user requests that are completely served by a monolith do not have to make many calls across a network

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:51.809
Which reduces the chance of Network failures

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:56.339
Most software companies have their code in a monolith today

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:00.480
When those monoliths get big problems can start to occur

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:05.099
Centralized code leads to tight couplings that are hard to break up

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:10.260
If a program is too big it will be impossible to run on a typical machine

00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:16.258
Internet giants in the early 2000s began breaking up their applications into services

00:01:16.869 --> 00:01:19.709
Instead of scaling the monolithic application

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:24.720
a service-oriented architecture could scale the parts of an application that were under load

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:31.020
Operating system virtualization made service-oriented architecture more economical

00:01:32.049 --> 00:01:39.839
One server could host multiple virtualized operating system instances and each of those instances could run a service

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:47.279
But this also meant that engineers had to manage more and more layers of infrastructure the virtual machine hosts

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.680
the hypervisor layer and

00:01:49.899 --> 00:01:51.899
the hardware itself

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:54.210
Failures became more complex

00:01:54.729 --> 00:01:56.729
Debugging got harder

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:01.409
In 2006 Amazon Web Services launched the Elastic Compute cloud

00:02:02.829 --> 00:02:10.799
Ec2 allows programmers to rent virtual machines in Amazon's data centers. With Amazon taking care of failures at the hardware level

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:13.610
and the hypervisor level

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:19.650
Programmers could focus on the virtual machine hosts themselves where their application code was running

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:26.009
But using an entire virtual machine to run a small piece of application code is wasteful

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:33.119
Containers allow a virtual machine to be sliced up into isolated filesystem regions

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:41.940
A container can be as large as the entire VM or as small as your smallest service. Hence the term micro services

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:49.980
Micro services run in containers which run on a virtual machine which runs on a hypervisor which runs on a server

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:57.209
Which sits in Iraq which sits in a data center, which is part of a network of data centers called the cloud

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:00.509
Containerized architecture it led to a new problem

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:07.350
Companies that ran thousands of micro services in containers on the cloud did not have a simple way of managing them

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:15.570
Kubernetes is an open source project from Google that gives engineers a centralized system for managing containers

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:19.169
Kubernetes also makes those services portable

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:24.209
Creating a competitive tension between Amazon Web Services and Google cloud platform

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:27.720
both of which can host kubernetes clusters

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:30.959
The days of micro services are just getting started

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:38.250
Software development has never been easier and the two biggest companies in the cloud are competing for users

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:41.620
It's going to keep getting easier and cheaper

